) is tangent to a conic section with focus \\( F \\), so the answer is \\boxed{\\text{a conic section with focus } F}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given that \\( F \\) is a focus, and \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) are two moving points on a conic section such that \\(\\angle PFQ = 2\\delta\\) (where \\(\\delta\\) is an acute angle), we need to prove the following:\n\n1. The locus of the intersection of lines passing through points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) is a conic section with focus \\( F \\).\n2. The line \\( PQ \\) is always tangent to a conic section with focus \\( F \\).\n\n### Key Steps and Reasoning:\n\n1. **Locus of Intersection of Lines through \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\)**:\n   - Consider the original conic section with focus \\( F \\). Points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) move on this conic such that \\(\\angle PFQ = 2\\delta\\).\n   - The lines passing through \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) considered are the tangents at these points. The intersection of these tangents, say \\( X \\), forms a locus.\n   - Using the properties of conic sections, specifically the polar equation of a conic and the angle condition, it is shown that the locus of \\( X \\) is another conic section. This is derived by considering the intersection of tangents at \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) which satisfy the angle condition \\(\\angle PFQ = 2\\delta\\).\n   - The intersection of these tangents results in a circle (a special case of a conic section) when the original conic is a circle, and this generalizes to other conic sections through projective transformations and properties.\n\n2. **Line \\( PQ \\) as Tangent to a Conic Section**:\n   - The line \\( PQ \\) is the chord of the original conic section. Using the concept of pole and polar with respect to a conic section, the line \\( PQ \\) is the polar of the intersection point \\( X \\) of the tangents at \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\).\n   - Since the locus of \\( X \\) is a conic section (as proven in part 1), the envelope of the polars (lines \\( PQ \\)) of points on this conic section is also a conic section. This follows from the duality principle in projective geometry, where the envelope of polars of points on a conic is another conic.\n\n### Final Answer\n\n1. The locus of the intersection of lines passing through points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) is a conic section with focus \\( F \\).  \n   \\boxed{\\text{a conic section with focus } F}\n\n2. The line \\( PQ \\) is always tangent to a conic section with focus \\( F \\).  \n   \\boxed{\\text{a conic section with focus } F}", "ground_truth_solution": "Given that $F$ is the focus, $P$ and $Q$ are two moving points on the ellipse, and $\\angle PFQ = 2\\delta$ where $\\delta$ is an acute angle.\n\n#### *Proof of Part (I)*:\n\n1. **Polar Coordinates Setup**:\n    - Consider the polar coordinate system with $F$ as the pole.\n    - Let the polar angles of points $P$ and $Q$ be $\\gamma + \\delta$ and $\\gamma - \\delta$ respectively, where $\\gamma$ is a parameter.\n  \n2. **Equation of the Line Passing Through $P$ and $Q$**:\n    - The polar equation for the line passing through $P$ and $Q$ in terms of their polar angles can be written as:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{ep}{\\rho} = \\cos(\\theta - \\gamma - \\delta) - e \\cos \\theta \\quad \\text{(1)}\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      \\frac{ep}{\\rho} = \\cos(\\theta - \\gamma + \\delta) - e \\cos \\theta \\quad \\text{(2)}\n      \\]\n\n3. **Equating Both Equations**:\n    - From equations (1) and (2), we obtain:\n      \\[\n      \\cos(\\theta - \\gamma - \\delta) = \\cos(\\theta - \\gamma + \\delta)\n      \\]\n\n4. **Solving for $\\theta$**:\n    - Since $\\delta \\neq 0$, the equality $\\cos(\\theta - \\gamma - \\delta) = \\cos(\\theta - \\gamma + \\delta)$ implies that $\\theta = \\gamma$.\n\n5. **Substituting $\\gamma = \\theta$**:\n    - Substituting $\\gamma = \\theta$ into equation (1), we get:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{ep \\cdot \\sec \\delta}{\\rho} = 1 - e \\sec \\delta \\cdot \\cos \\theta\n      \\]\n\n6. **Resulting Curve**:\n    - The resulting equation has the form of a conic section with $F$ as the focus, $e \\sec \\delta$ as the eccentricity, and $2ep \\sec \\delta$ as the focal parameter. Based on the value of $e \\sec \\delta$, the conic section can be:\n        - An ellipse if $e \\sec \\delta < 1$\n        - A parabola if $e \\sec \\delta = 1$\n        - A hyperbola if $e \\sec \\delta > 1$\n\n    Thus, the locus of the intersection of lines passing through $P$ and $Q$ is a conic with $F$ as the focus.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Part (I)}}\n\\]\n\n#### *Proof of Part (II)*:\n\n1. **Equation of Line $PQ$**:\n    - The equation of the line passing through $P$ and $Q$ can also be written as:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{ep}{\\rho} = \\sec \\delta \\cdot \\cos(\\theta - \\gamma) - e \\cos \\theta\n      \\]\n    - This simplifies to:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{ep \\cdot \\cos \\delta}{\\rho} = \\cos(\\theta - \\gamma) - e \\cos \\delta \\cdot \\cos \\theta \\quad \\text{(3)}\n      \\]\n\n2. **Comparing With Standard Form**:\n    - Comparing equation (3) with the standard form of the equation of a conic section:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{ep \\cos \\delta}{\\rho} = 1 - e \\cos \\delta \\cdot \\cos \\theta\n      \\]\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n    - From the comparison, it is clear that the line $PQ$ is a tangent to the conic section defined by the equation with $e \\cos \\delta$ as the eccentricity. Therefore, $PQ$ is tangent to a conic section with its focus at $F$.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Part (II)}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Inside the triangle \\(ABC\\) there is a given point \\(M\\). Prove that\n\n$$\n\\frac{S_{ACM} + S_{BCM}}{S_{ABM}} = \\frac{CM}{C_{1}M}\n$$\n\nwhere \\(C_{1}\\) is the intersection point of the lines \\(AB\\) and \\(CM\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that inside triangle ABC, with a point M, the ratio of the sum of the areas of triangles ACM and BCM to the area of tri