 2 \\cdot 136 = 1856 - 272 = 1584.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\]\n\n    Observing patterns:\n    \\[\n    c_5 = 464 = 16 \\times 29, \\quad c_6 = 1584 = 16 \\times 99\n    \\]\n    Assume \\( k \\geq 5 \\) and \\( 16 \\mid c_k \\) and \\( 16 \\mid c_{k+1} \\). It follows from recurrence relation:\n    \\[\n    c_{k+2} = 4c_{k+1} - 2c_k \\implies 16 \\mid c_{k+2}\n    \\]\n\n    Therefore, for \\( k \\geq 5 \\), \\( 16 \\mid c_m \\). And for \\( b_m = 4^m \\), \\( 16 \\mid b_m \\) for \\( m \\geq 2 \\).\n\n5. **Divisibility by 7**\n    \n    Next, show \\( 7 \\mid a_m \\). Check:\n    \\[\n    b_{m+3} = 64b_m \\equiv b_m \\pmod{7}\n    \\]\n    And for \\( c_{m+3} \\):\n    \\[\n    c_{m+3} = 4c_{m+2} - 2c_{m+1} \\implies -3 \\cdot (c_{m+2} = 4c_{m+1} - 2c_m) \\implies  c_{m+3} \\equiv -c_m \\pmod{7}\n    \\]\n\n    Hence:\n    \\[\n    c_{m+6} \\equiv c_m \\pmod{7}\n    \\]\n\n    Which results in:\n    \\[\n    a_{m+6} = b_{m+6} - c_{m+6} \\equiv b_m - c_m = a_m \\pmod{7}\n    \\]\n\n    Direct computation for small \\( m \\):\n    \\[\n    a_0 = 1 - 2 = -1, \\quad a_1 = 4-4 = 0, \\quad a_2 = 16-12 = 4, \\quad a_3 = 64-40 = 24, \\quad a_4 = 256-136 = 120, \\quad a_5 = 1024-464 = 560\n    \\]\n\n    Observing \\( 7 \\mid a_1 \\) and \\( 7 \\mid a_5 \\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**\n    For \\( m = 1 \\) or \\( m = 6k+1 \\) or \\( m = 6k+5 \\) with \\( k \\geq 0 \\), giving:\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{112 \\mid a_m \\text{ if and only if \\( m \\) is a non-divisible by 3 positive odd integer.}}\n    \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the number of ordered integer pairs \\((a, b)\\) such that the equation \\(x^{2} + a x + b = 167 y\\) has integer solutions \\((x, y)\\), where \\(1 \\leq a, b \\leq 2004\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the number of ordered integer pairs (a, b) where 1 \u2264 a, b \u2264 2004, and the equation x\u00b2 + a x + b = 167 y has integer solutions (x, y). Hmm, l