So all variables are at least 2. Then the maximum possible sum is 4*(1/4) = 1, which is achieved when all are 2. So perhaps (2,2,2,2) is the only solution. But that seems too straightforward. Maybe there are other solutions with some variables larger than 2 and some equal to 2? Let's check.\n\nSuppose we have three variables as 2, and one variable as something else. Let's call the fourth variable t. Then the equation becomes 3*(1/4) + 1/t\u00b2 = 1. So 3/4 + 1/t\u00b2 = 1 \u21d2 1/t\u00b2 = 1/4 \u21d2 t\u00b2 = 4 \u21d2 t = 2. But that just gives all variables as 2 again. So no other solutions in this case.\n\nWhat if two variables are 2, and the other two variables are something else. Let's denote them as a and b. Then the equation becomes 2*(1/4) + 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1 \u21d2 1/2 + 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1 \u21d2 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2. Now, we need to find positive integers a, b \u2265 2 such that 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2. Let's explore this.\n\nAssume without loss of generality that a \u2264 b. Then a can be 2, 3, 4, etc. Let's try a = 2: then 1/4 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2 \u21d2 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2 - 1/4 = 1/4 \u21d2 b\u00b2 = 4 \u21d2 b = 2. So that gives a = 2, b = 2, which leads back to the solution (2,2,2,2). If a = 3: 1/9 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2 \u21d2 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2 - 1/9 = 7/18 \u21d2 b\u00b2 = 18/7, which is not an integer. So no solution here. If a = 4: 1/16 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2 \u21d2 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2 - 1/16 = 7/16 \u21d2 b\u00b2 = 16/7 \u2248 2.285, not integer. a = 5: 1/25 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2 \u21d2 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2 - 1/25 = 23/50 \u21d2 b\u00b2 = 50/23 \u2248 2.173, nope. a = 6: 1/36 + 1/b\u00b2 = 1/2 \u21d2 1/b\u00b2 = 17/36 \u21d2 b\u00b2 = 36/17 \u2248 2.117, not integer. So no solutions here. Therefore, if we have two variables as 2, the other two must also be 2. So the only solution in this case is all variables being 2.\n\nAlternatively, what if only one variable is 2, and the other three are greater than or equal to 3. Then the equation becomes 1/4 + 