t solution to factor out terms.\n\nBut maybe this is overcomplicating. Since we found one solution through integer guessing, and the other possibilities for z didn't yield solutions, perhaps this is the only solution. To confirm, maybe check another possible approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's try to express variables in terms of x.\n\nFrom equation1: y = -3 -x\u00b2 +2z\n\nFrom equation2:3x + y + z\u00b2=14. Substitute y:\n\n3x + (-3 -x\u00b2 +2z) + z\u00b2=14\n\n\u2192 -x\u00b2 +3x +2z + z\u00b2 -3 -14=0\n\n\u2192 -x\u00b2 + z\u00b2 +3x +2z -17=0 \u2192 same as equation (A)\n\nFrom equation3:7x - y\u00b2 +4z=25. Substitute y:\n\n7x - (-3 -x\u00b2 +2z)^2 +4z=25\n\nWhich is the same as before. If we assume that x=2, z=3, then let's see:\n\nFrom equation (A): - (2)^2 + (3)^2 +3*(2) +2*(3) -17= -4 +9 +6 +6 -17= ( -4 +9)=5, (5+6)=11, (11+6)=17, (17 -17)=0. So satisfies equation (A).\n\nIf we try x=2 in the expression for z: z= -1 \u00b1 sqrt(x\u00b2 -3x +18)= -1 \u00b1 sqrt(4 -6 +18)=sqrt(16)=4. So z=-1 \u00b14. So z=3 or z=-5. Wait, z=3 is the solution we found. What about z=-5?\n\nIf z=-5, then from equation (C): z=-5, so equation (C): z\u00b2 +2z =25 -10=15. But x\u00b2 -3x +17 must equal 15. So x\u00b2 -3x +17=15\u2192x\u00b2 -3x +2=0\u2192(x-1)(x-2)=0\u2192x=1 or x=2.\n\nSo x=1 or x=2. Let's check x=1, z=-5.\n\nFrom equation1: y= -3 -1 +2*(-5)= -4 -10= -14.\n\nCheck equation2:3*1 + (-14) + (-5)^2=3 -14 +25=14. Correct.\n\nCheck equation3:7*1 - (-14)^2 +4*(-5)=7 -196 -20= -209\u226025. Not valid.\n\nx=2, z=-5:\n\nFrom equation1:y= -3 -4 +2*(-5)= -7 -10= -17.\n\nCheck equation2:3*2 +(-17) +25=6 -17 +25=14. Correct.\n\nCheck equation3:7*2 - (-17)^2 +4*(-5)=14 -289 -20= -295\u226025. Not valid.\n\nSo z=-5 gives solutions that satisfy equations 1 and 2 but not 3. Therefore, they are not valid.\n\nHence, the only solution from this is z=3, x=2, y=-1.\n\nAlternatively, let's suppose there's another real solution. Let's take x=3, just to check.\n\nFrom equation2:3x + y +z\u