re no integer solutions for the given equation.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a trapezoid with bases respectively equal to \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) (\\(a > b\\)), a line parallel to the bases is drawn through the intersection point of the diagonals. Find the length of the segment of this line intercepted by the non-parallel sides of the trapezoid.\n\nGiven (Fig. 33): \\(ABCD\\) is a trapezoid, \\(|AD| = a\\), \\(|BC| = b\\), \\([AC]\\) and \\([BD]\\) are diagonals, \\((PQ) \\parallel (AD)\\), \\(M \\in (PQ)\\), \\(M = [AC] \\cap [BD]\\), \\(P \\in [AB]\\), \\(Q \\in [CD]\\).\n\nFind: \\(|PQ|\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to find the length of segment PQ in a trapezoid ABCD where the bases are AD = a and BC = b, with a > b. The line PQ is drawn through the intersection point M of the diagonals AC and BD, and PQ is parallel to the bases AD and BC. Hmm, okay. Let me start by recalling some properties of trapezoids and their diagonals.\n\nFirst, in a trapezoid, the diagonals intersect each other in such a way that the product of the segments of one diagonal is equal to the product of the segments of the other diagonal. But I might be mixing that with another figure. Wait, no, maybe in a trapezoid, the ratio of the segments created by the intersection of the diagonals is equal to the ratio of the lengths of the two bases. Let me check that.\n\nSuppose diagonals AC and BD intersect at point M. Then, the ratio of AM to MC should be equal to the ratio of the lengths of the bases AD to BC. Since AD is a and BC is b, then AM/MC = a/b. Similarly, BM/MD should also be equal to a/b. Is that correct? Let me think. If we have two triangles formed by the diagonals, maybe similar triangles? Because the bases are parallel, so the triangles formed by the diagonals might be similar. \n\nYes, actually, triangles AMD and BMC should be similar because AD is parallel to BC. Therefore, the ratio of