 we think of \\( a \\) as a function of \\( x \\), then to have the original equation have a unique solution, the horizontal line \\( a = \\text{constant} \\) must be tangent to the curve \\( a(x) = \\frac{x^2}{2(x + \\ln x)} \\). Therefore, the value of \\( a \\) would be the extremum (minimum or maximum) of this function.\n\nTherefore, to find such \\( a \\), we can take the derivative of \\( a(x) \\) with respect to \\( x \\), set it to zero, solve for \\( x \\), and then compute \\( a \\).\n\nLet me try this approach.\n\nLet \\( a(x) = \\frac{x^2}{2(x + \\ln x)} \\). Compute \\( a'(x) \\):\n\nUsing the quotient rule:\n\n\\( a'(x) = \\frac{[2x \\cdot 2(x + \\ln x) - x^2 \\cdot (2(1 + \\frac{1}{x}))]}{[2(x + \\ln x)]^2} \\)\n\nWait, no. Wait, the quotient rule is \\( (f/g)' = (f' g - f g') / g^2 \\).\n\nSo, let me correct that.\n\nLet \\( f(x) = x^2 \\), \\( g(x) = 2(x + \\ln x) \\). Then,\n\n\\( a(x) = f(x)/g(x) \\)\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\( a'(x) = [f'(x) g(x) - f(x) g'(x)] / [g(x)]^2 \\)\n\nCompute \\( f'(x) = 2x \\)\n\nCompute \\( g'(x) = 2(1 + 1/x) \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( a'(x) = [2x * 2(x + \\ln x) - x^2 * 2(1 + 1/x)] / [2(x + \\ln x)]^2 \\)\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\nFirst term: \\( 2x * 2(x + \\ln x) = 4x(x + \\ln x) \\)\n\nSecond term: \\( x^2 * 2(1 + 1/x) = 2x^2(1 + 1/x) = 2x^2 + 2x \\)\n\nTherefore, numerator:\n\n\\( 4x(x + \\ln x) - 2x^2 - 2x = 4x^2 + 4x \\ln x - 2x^2 - 2x = 2x^2 + 4x \\ln x - 2x \\)\n\nFactor numerator:\n\n\\( 2x(x + 2 \\ln x - 1) \\)\n\nTherefore, numerator is \\( 2x(x + 2 \\ln x - 1) \\), denominator is \\( [2(x + \\ln x)]^2 = 4(x + \\ln x)^2 \\)\n\nThus,\n\n\\( a'(x) = [2x(x + 2 \\ln x - 1)] / [4(x + \\ln x)^2] = [x(x + 2 \\ln x - 1)] / [2(x + \\ln x)^2] \\)\n\nSet \\( a'(x) = 0 \\):\n\nThe numerator must be zero:\n\n\\( x(x + 2 \\ln x - 1) = 0 \\)\n\nSince \\( x > 0 \\), \\( x \\neq 0 \\), so:\n\n\\( x + 2 \\ln x - 1 = 0 \\)\n\nWhich is exactly equation (3) that we had earlier! So, solving \\( x + 2 \\ln x - 1 = 0 \\), which we found \\( x 