ywhere, maybe even fewer are needed, but the question is to show that floor(n/3) is sufficient regardless. The standard proof uses vertex guards, which are a subset of all possible guard positions. Therefore, if floor(n/3) vertex guards are sufficient, then certainly floor(n/3) guards in general are sufficient. So, that approach still works. \n\nSo, the steps are:\n\n1. Triangulate the polygon into n-2 triangles.\n2. Construct the dual graph, which is a tree.\n3. 3-color the vertices of the polygon such that no two adjacent vertices (in the triangulated graph) share the same color.\n4. Choose the smallest color class; since there are three colors, the smallest class has at most floor(n/3) vertices.\n5. Place guards at these vertices. Since every triangle has one vertex of each color, each triangle is monitored by the guard at its colored vertex. Therefore, the entire polygon is covered.\n\nBut let me verify why each triangle being covered implies the whole polygon is covered. If a guard is placed at a vertex, then they can see all points in the polygon for which the line segment from the guard to the point doesn't intersect any walls. In a triangle, a guard at a vertex can see the entire triangle because the triangle is convex. Since the polygon is partitioned into triangles, each triangle is entirely visible from its colored vertex. Therefore, the entire polygon is covered. \n\nHowever, I need to ensure that visibility through adjacent triangles is handled. For example, even though a guard is in one triangle, can they see into adjacent triangles? Wait, no, because the triangulation divides the polygon into triangles with diagonals. So, the guard at a vertex can see the entire triangle they're in, but the adjacent triangles are separated by a diagonal. However, the guard is at a vertex shared by multiple triangles. So, a guard at a vertex can see all triangles that include that vertex. Therefore, if the original triangulation is such that all triangles incident to a