took the same number of candies.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a chess tournament, $n$ players played a round-robin tournament. Players A and B each played two games before withdrawing from the tournament. It is known that a total of 81 games were played. Determine the value of $n$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this chess tournament problem. So, the problem says there are n players in a round-robin tournament. Players A and B each played two games before withdrawing. The total number of games played in the tournament is 81. We need to find the value of n.\n\nFirst, let me recall what a round-robin tournament is. In a round-robin tournament, each player plays against every other player exactly once. So, normally, the total number of games in a round-robin tournament with n players would be C(n, 2), which is n(n-1)/2. But in this case, two players, A and B, withdrew after each played two games. So the total number of games played is less than the full round-robin number.\n\nWait, but the total games played are given as 81. So, the regular number of games minus the games that weren't played because A and B withdrew. Hmm. Or maybe not exactly, because they withdrew after playing two games each. So, we need to calculate how many games were actually played considering their early withdrawal.\n\nLet me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, if all n players had completed the round-robin tournament, the total number of games would be n(n-1)/2. But since A and B withdrew early, some games were not played. Specifically, each player usually plays (n-1) games, but A and B only played two games each. So, the number of games they didn't play is (n-1 - 2) each. But since each game involves two players, maybe the total number of unplayed games is (n-1 - 2) + (n-1 - 2) divided by 1? Wait, no, because each unplayed game between A and someone else is a single game. Similarly for B