nation formula:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{n-2}{2} = \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. We are given that a total of 81 matches were played. Considering two possible scenarios:\n   - Scenario 1: Participants \\( \\text{A} \\) and \\( \\text{B} \\) did not play each other.\n   - Scenario 2: Participants \\( \\text{A} \\) and \\( \\text{B} \\) played each other once.\n\n#### Scenario 1: \\( \\text{A} \\) and \\( \\text{B} \\) did not play each other.\nIn this case, we add the individual matches of \\( \\text{A} \\) and \\( \\text{B} \\) to the combination of \\( n-2 \\) participants:\n\\[\n\\binom{n-2}{2} + 2 \\times 2 = 81\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Rightarrow \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2} + 4 = 81\n\\]\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2} = 77\n\\]\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\\[\n(n-2)(n-3) = 154\n\\]\nSolve the quadratic equation:\n\\[\nn^2 - 5n + 6 = 154\n\\]\n\\[\nn^2 - 5n - 148 = 0\n\\]\nThis quadratic equation has no integer solutions.\n\n#### Scenario 2: \\( \\text{A} \\) and \\( \\text{B} \\) played each other.\nIn this case, we add 3 to the combination of \\( n-2 \\) participants matches:\n\\[\n\\binom{n-2}{2} + 3 = 81\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Rightarrow \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2} + 3 = 81\n\\]\nSubtract 3 from both sides:\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2} = 78\n\\]\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\\[\n(n-2)(n-3) = 156\n\\]\nSolve the quadratic equation:\n\\[\nn^2 - 5n + 6 = 156\n\\]\n\\[\nn^2 - 5n - 150 = 0\n\\]\nUse the quadratic formula \\( n = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -5 \\), and \\( c = -150 \\):\n\\[\nn = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 + 600}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nn = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{625}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nn = \\frac{5 \\pm 25}{2}\n\\]\nThus, we have two possible solutions:\n\\[\nn = 15 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad n = -10\n\\]\nSince \\( n \\) must be a positive integer,\n\\[\nn = 15\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe number of participants \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null