 tangency.\n\nSuppose the right leg is tangent to the circle at point (x1, y1). Since the leg is tangent to the circle at (x1, y1), the equation of the tangent line at that point is x1 x + y1 y = r\u00b2. But also, this line connects the top base (y = r) to the bottom base (y = -r). Let's find the coordinates where the tangent line meets the top and bottom bases.\n\nAt y = r, substituting into the tangent line equation: x1 x + y1 r = r\u00b2. Solving for x: x = (r\u00b2 - y1 r)/x1. Similarly, at y = -r: x1 x + y1 (-r) = r\u00b2 \u21d2 x = (r\u00b2 + y1 r)/x1.\n\nSo the two endpoints of the right leg are at ((r\u00b2 - y1 r)/x1, r) and ((r\u00b2 + y1 r)/x1, -r). The distance between these two points is the length of the leg, which we can compute. But since the trapezoid is isosceles, the legs are symmetric, so the left leg will have endpoints mirrored across the y-axis.\n\nBut maybe there's a simpler approach. Let's consider the point of tangency on the leg. Since the trapezoid is isosceles, the legs are symmetric, so the points of tangency on the legs will be symmetric with respect to the y-axis. Let's denote the points of tangency on the right leg as (a, b) and on the left leg as (-a, b). Wait, but if the circle is centered at the origin, and the trapezoid is symmetric about the y-axis, then the points of tangency on the legs would actually be symmetric across the y-axis. However, since each leg is on one side (right and left), the points of tangency on each leg would be mirror images. Wait, maybe both points of tangency on the legs are on the same horizontal line? Hmm, perhaps not. Wait, the legs are slanting sides, so the points of tangency would be different.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the distance between the two points of tangency on the legs is the chord that connects them. If the two points are on the circle, then the distance between them is \u221a3 r. So given two points on the circle, separated by a distance of \u221a3 r. Let me find the angle between them